  <div class="topic-divider"><h3>📐 Orthogonal Cutting Model</h3></div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3><span class="icon icon-green">✂️</span>Chip Formation <span class="badge badge-exam">EXAM</span></h3>
    <p>Orthogonal model reduces 3D cuts to 2D. The cutting edge is perpendicular to motion.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Chip thickness ratio (r):</strong> <code>r = t_o / t_c</code> (always &lt; 1 because chip gets thicker).</li>
      <li><strong>Shear angle (φ):</strong> <code>tan φ = r cos α / (1 - r sin α)</code></li>
      <li><strong>Shear strain (γ):</strong> <code>γ = tan(φ - α) + cot φ</code></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  
  <div class="topic-divider"><h3>⚖️ Force &amp; Merchant Equation</h3></div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3><span class="icon icon-blue">⚖️</span>Forces in Machining</h3>
    <p>Only <strong>Cutting Force (F_c)</strong> does work. <strong>Thrust force (F_t)</strong> causes deflection.</p>
    <p><strong>Merchant Equation:</strong> <code>φ = 45° + α/2 - β/2</code></p>
    <div class="tip">To get a cheaper cut (lower power), you want a <strong>larger shear angle (φ)</strong>. Achieve this by increasing rake angle (α) or reducing friction (β).</div>
  </div>
  
  <div class="topic-divider"><h3>⚡ Power &amp; Energy</h3></div>
  <div class="card">
    <h3><span class="icon icon-yellow">⚡</span>Cutting Power</h3>
    <pre>P_c = F_c * v
P_g = P_c / E (Gross Power, E = efficiency)</pre>
    <p><strong>Specific Energy (U):</strong> Energy required to remove 1 unit volume of material. <code>P_c = U * R_MR</code></p>
    <div class="warn">As uncut chip thickness (t_o) falls, specific energy <strong>rises</strong> (Size Effect).</div>
  </div>
