    <ul>
      <li><strong>Peripheral (Plain) Milling:</strong> Cutter axis is parallel to surface. Outside edges do the cutting.</li>
      <li><strong>Face Milling:</strong> Cutter axis is perpendicular to surface. Edges on both end and periphery cut.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Feed Rate:</strong> <code>f_r = N * n_t * f</code> (where n_t = number of teeth)</p>
    <div class="warn">Don't forget the <strong>approach distance (A)</strong> in milling time calculations! <code>T_m = (L + A) / f_r</code></div>
  </div>
  
  <div class="card">
    <h3><span class="icon icon-purple">⬆️</span>Up vs Down Milling</h3>
    <p><strong>Up Milling:</strong> Teeth move against feed. Chip starts thin, grows thick. Tendency to lift work.</p>
    <p><strong>Down (Climb) Milling:</strong> Teeth move with feed. Chip starts thick, thins out. Pushes work down (simpler fixturing, better finish).</p>
  </div>
